---
// Sources and licences of the Genesis Reader (src/pages/sources.astro).
import { KETIV_QERE, SUFFIX_LEMMAS } from "./alignment";
import { LEXICON_COMMIT, OSHB_COMMIT } from "./sources";

const ketivs = Object.keys(KETIV_QERE).map((id) => id.replace(".", ":"));
const pronouns = Object.keys(SUFFIX_LEMMAS).length;
---
<h2>Hebrew</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>The Westminster Leningrad Codex (WLC), Genesis 1–11</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain. The text is the Leningrad Codex as the Open Scriptures Hebrew Bible (OSHB) encodes it in <a href="https://github.com/openscriptures/morphhb">openscriptures/morphhb</a> (<code>wlc/Gen.xml</code>,
    pinned to commit <code>{OSHB_COMMIT}</code>), in the Masoretic verse numbering. It is
    printed as OSHB has it, without normalization: the letters, vowel points, <i>dagesh</i>, <i>meteg</i>, <i>maqqef</i>,
    <i>paseq</i> and <i>sof pasuq</i>. The cantillation accents (the <i>te‘amim</i>, U+0591–U+05AF) are left out,
    because they stack on the vowel points and are hard to read; search ignores the points as well, so a query in bare
    consonants finds the pointed word. The open and closed paragraph marks of the manuscript (<i>petuhah</i>,
    <i>setumah</i>) are printed as <span lang="hbo" dir="rtl">{"{פ}"}</span> and <span lang="hbo" dir="rtl">{"{ס}"}</span>.
    Words joined by a <i>maqqef</i> are separate words, each clickable.
  </dd>
  <dt>Open Scriptures Hebrew Bible, lemmas and morphology</dt>
  <dd>
    Every word’s lemma and grammatical analysis come from the OSHB Parsing Project (<a href="https://hb.openscriptures.org/">hb.openscriptures.org</a>),
    licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. Original work of the Open Scriptures
    Hebrew Bible available at <a href="https://github.com/openscriptures/morphhb">github.com/openscriptures/morphhb</a>.
    The analysis is the volunteer project’s, checked by its editors, not a single scholar’s treebank.
  </dd>
  <dt>Morphology</dt>
  <dd>
    OSHB splits a written word into its morphemes, and the word panel gives each its own card: a prefixed conjunction,
    preposition, article or relative <span lang="hbo" dir="rtl">שֶׁ</span>, the stem, and a pronominal suffix.
    OSHB’s codes are mapped to Universal Dependencies, with the Hebrew verbal stem as <code>HebBinyan</code>
    (<i>Qal</i> is OSHB’s <i>PAAL</i>, <i>Hiphil</i> is <i>HIFIL</i>) and the conjugation as aspect and mood: perfect,
    imperfect, <i>wayyiqtol</i> and <i>weqatal</i>, participle, infinitive. A preposition with the article fused into its
    vowels (<span lang="hbo" dir="rtl">בַּ</span>, <span lang="hbo" dir="rtl">לַ</span>) is shown as two cards,
    preposition and article. A pronominal suffix has no lemma in OSHB; it takes the independent pronoun of its person,
    gender and number ({pronouns} are used), and the directional and paragogic <i>he</i> and <i>nun</i> have entries of
    their own. Entries that share a headword are numbered (<span lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אֵת</span>¹ the sign of the
    accusative, <span lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אֵת</span>² “with”).
  </dd>
  <dt>Ketiv and qere</dt>
  <dd>
    Where the manuscript writes one word and the Masoretes direct the reader to say another, OSHB analyses both. The
    reader prints the word as it is read (<i>qere</i>), with its pointing, and notes the consonants as written
    (<i>ketiv</i>) in the word’s card. In Genesis 1–11 this is {ketivs.join(", ")} only
    (<span lang="hbo" dir="rtl">הוצא</span> written, <span lang="hbo" dir="rtl">הַיְצֵא</span> read).
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>English</h2>
<dl>
  <dt><i>The Holy Scriptures according to the Masoretic Text</i>, Jewish Publication Society of America (1917)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain (<a href="https://ebible.org/engjps/copyright.htm">eBible.org’s copyright statement</a>). The text is
    eBible.org’s USFM edition, <a href="https://ebible.org/Scriptures/engjps_usfm.zip">engjps_usfm.zip</a>, one line
    to a verse. eBible regenerates its archive, so the archive cannot be hash-pinned: the build pins the
    extracted <code>02-GENengjps.usfm</code> by its SHA-256 and stops if the text changes. JPS follows the Hebrew
    verses one for one in Genesis 1–11, which the build checks. The straight quotation marks of the file are printed as
    typographic ones. The print sets the first words of the book in small capitals, and eBible copies them as capitals
    (“IN THE beginning”); here they read “In the beginning”, and the build stops if any other verse opens that way.
    The LORD of the Tetragrammaton stays in capitals. Nothing else is changed.
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>Dictionaries</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Francis Brown, S. R. Driver and Charles A. Briggs, <i>A Hebrew and English Lexicon of the Old Testament</i> (1906)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain, as encoded by the Open Scriptures <a href="https://github.com/openscriptures/HebrewLexicon">Hebrew Lexicon</a> (commit <code>{LEXICON_COMMIT}</code>),
    whose compilation and indexes are licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>.
    Each OSHB lemma number is joined to its BDB entry through the project’s <code>AugIndex.xml</code> and
    <code>LexicalIndex.xml</code>. The word panel shows the entry’s numbered senses, flattened to two levels, with the
    verbal stems on lines of their own. Many entries (most proper names) have no definition in the Open Scriptures
    encoding of BDB; they show BDB’s grammar line with the index’s gloss in italics. The lexicon list gives each
    lemma’s first definitions from BDB, or that gloss.
  </dd>
  <dt>James Strong, <i>Strong’s Hebrew Dictionary</i> (1894)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain, as the Open Scriptures lexical index carries it. Strong’s numbers (H853 …) identify the lemmas in
    OSHB; the “OSHB lexicon” line of each word’s entry gives the number with the index’s transliteration and
    one-word gloss. The index’s TWOT numbers are not used.
  </dd>
</dl>
